INSERT INTO feature VALUES('boot-alloc','runtime','Memory from the kernel with no libc','bootstrap','partial','notable',NULL,'The crate `gandr-boot-alloc` maps whole pages of anonymous memory, changes their protection and gives them back. A mapper holds the size of a page, which the caller states, and a request names a protection and whether the host reserves memory for the pages. No protection runs code. On Linux each call is a system call by the crate''s own function in assembly, on x86-64, aarch64 and riscv64, and on macOS each call is a declared function of the system library. A heap for one thread cuts blocks in size classes from 16 bytes to 4 KiB out of runs of 64 KiB, and a larger block takes whole pages of its own. The heap is an allocator of Rust''s `alloc` through a shared reference, and its end gives each run back. The emitter maps the address space of its regions through the crate. One allocation with one free of 64 bytes takes 3.8 ns on macOS arm64, 7.4 ns on Linux x86-64 and 41 ns on Linux riscv64 at the median. The emitter''s executable memory (#8111) and its reads of the page size and the physical memory (#8112) still go through libc, and no twin in gandr replaces the crate yet (#7833).','boot-alloc','crates/boot-alloc/tests/map.rs crates/boot-alloc/tests/heap.rs crates/boot-alloc/tests/pages.rs','crate-tests','#7833 #8111 #8112');
